  "question": {
    "text": "An isolated particle of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math> is moving in the horizontal plane <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> along the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math>-axis, at a certain height above the ground. It suddenly explodes into two fragments of masses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>.&nbsp;An instant later, the smaller fragment is at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. The larger fragment at this instant is at",
    "image": null
  },
